  "requestTimestamp": "2026-07-19T01:29:27.167Z",
  "answer": "### Short Answer\nYes, the employee is required to file an annual Income Tax Return (ITR) if they derived compensation concurrently from two or more employers during the taxable year.\n\n### Controlling Authorities\nThis requirement is established under Section 51 of the National Internal Revenue Code (NIRC), which states that \"an individual deriving compensation concurrently from two or more employers at any time during the taxable year shall file an income tax return.\"\n\n### Interpretation\nThe provision clearly mandates that individuals who have multiple employers within a single taxable year must file an ITR, regardless of the amount of income earned. This is to ensure that all income is reported and taxed appropriately, as the withholding tax may not fully capture the total tax liability of the individual.\n\n### Practical Meaning\nFor employees with multiple employers, filing an ITR is essential to comply with tax obligations. This means they must gather all income statements from each employer and report their total income for the year. Failure to file could result in penalties or issues with the Bureau of Internal Revenue (BIR).",
